
Consider the regression setting with $\Yspace = \R$ and feature space $\Xspace = \R^p$.
Let the hypothesis space be the linear models:

$$
    \Hspace = \{ \fx = \thetav^T \xv \mid \thetav \in \R^p \}
$$
Suppose your loss function of interest is the L2 loss $\Lxy = \frac{1}{2} (y - \fx)^2$.
Consider the $L_1$-regularized empirical risk of a model $\fx$ (i.e., lasso regression) with $\lambda > 0$:
$$
    \riskrt = \risket + \lambda ||\thetav||_1 = \frac{1}{2} \sumin (\yi - \thetav^T \xi)^2 + \lambda \sumjp |\theta_j|
$$
Assume that $\Xmat^T \Xmat = \id$, which holds if $\Xmat$ has orthonormal columns.
Show that the minimizer $\thetavh_{\text{lasso}} = (\thetah_{\text{lasso},1}, \ldots, \thetah_{\text{lasso},p})^T$ is given by

$$
    \thetah_{\text{lasso},j} = \sign(\thetah_j) \max\{|\thetah_j| - \lambda, 0\} \quad j = 1, \ldots, p
$$

where $\thetavh = (\thetah_1, \ldots, \thetah_p)^T = (\Xmat^T \Xmat)^{-1} \Xmat^T \yv$ is the minimizer of the unregularized empirical risk (w.r.t.\ the L2 loss).
For this purpose, use the following steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item
        Derive that

        $$
            \argmin_{\thetav} \riskrt = \argmin_{\thetav} \sumjp (-\thetah_j \theta_j + \frac{\theta_j^2}{2} + \lambda |\theta_j|)
        $$

        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz
        \lz

    \item
        Note that the objective on the right-hand side of (a) can be written as $\sum_{j=1}^p g_j(\theta_j)$, where
        $$
            g_j(\theta_j) = -\thetah_j \theta_j + \frac{\theta_j^2}{2} + \lambda |\theta_j|
        $$

        What is the advantage of this representation if we seek the $\thetav$ with entries $\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_p$ minimizing $\riskrt$?

        \lz
        \lz
        \lz

    \item
        Consider first the case $\thetah_j > 0$ and infer that the minimizer $\theta_j^*$ of $g_j$ satisfies $\theta_j^* \geq 0$.

        Hint: Compare $g_j(\theta_j)$ and $g_j(-\theta_j)$ for $\theta_j \geq 0$.

        \lz
